/**
 * @mainpage Main Page
 *
 * # C++ 1-Wire library for embedded systems
 *
 * This is yet another 1-Wire library among many. :) Born out of a
 * private project, it has been continuously expanded with new features. The
 * library is written in modern C++ and features a modular design. It includes
 * its own hardware abstraction layer to make porting to other platforms as
 * easy as possible. Further functions and improvements will follow.
 *
 * An overview of the features:
 *
 * - Hardware abstraction for improved portability
 * - Provision of a 1-Wire interface
 * - Provision of device-independent functions
 * - Provision of device-specific functions
 * - Helper functions for various calculations and tasks
 * 
 * Currently supported devices:
 * 
 * - DS18B20 temperature sensor
 * - DS2438 Battery monitoring
 * 
 * # Handling
 * 
 * Furthermore, I aim to document the library as comprehensively and clearly as
 * possible, so that even beginners can work with it without frustration.
 *
 * All classes are documented for the end user. A small example is provided to
 * get started, demonstrating how to simply read data from a DS18B20.
 * 
 * # Participation
 * 
 * Anyone wishing to contribute to the library is very welcome. There are a few
 * guidelines to ensure a consistent overall appearance; a page dedicated to
 * the @ref styleguide is available for this purpose.
 * 
 * # Further interesting reading
 * 
 * - @ref architecture - Explanation of the library's structure
 * - @ref busphysics - Explanation and notes on bus physics
 * - @ref styleguide - Style guide standards for changes to the library
 *
 * @todo Use `std::array` for array processing to avoid raw pointers. This also
 * allows the array size to be passed to functions simultaneously. This makes
 * the interfaces type-safe and easier to use.
 */
